course: space
lesson: 25
title: Black holes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A black hole is not a hole and not a cosmic vacuum cleaner: it is a huge amount of mass squeezed into a tiny space, so its gravity is so strong that not even light can get out.
explain: Its edge is the event horizon, a point of no return: like a waterfall, you can paddle safely far upstream, but once you go over the edge you can never come back.
explain: Squeeze the whole Earth down to the size of a marble, about 9 millimetres across, and it would become a black hole.
explain: Far from a black hole its pull is just like that of any star of the same mass: if the Sun became a black hole, Earth would keep circling, only cold and dark.
explain: We cannot see black holes directly, but we see what they do: stars racing round nothing visible, gas glowing as it falls in, and ripples in space when two of them crash.
fact: A black hole is a place where so much mass is squeezed into a small space that its gravity lets nothing, not even light, escape.
fact: The edge of a black hole is called the event horizon: anything that crosses it can never come back out.
fact: Some very heavy stars collapse into black holes at the end of their lives, usually a few to a few dozen times the mass of the Sun; the Sun is far too light to become one and will end as a white dwarf.
fact: The nearest known black holes are more than a thousand light years away, far too far to harm us.
fact: Most large galaxies have a supermassive black hole at their centre, millions to billions of times the mass of the Sun.
fact: In 2019 the Event Horizon Telescope published the first image of a black hole's shadow, M87*, a dark centre in a glowing orange ring.
fact: If the Sun were replaced by a black hole of the same mass, Earth would keep orbiting just as before, only in the cold and dark.
fact: Falling feet-first towards a small black hole, the pull on your feet would be so much stronger than on your head that you would be stretched out like spaghetti.
fact: To become a black hole, Earth would have to be squeezed to about 9 millimetres across, about the size of a marble.
fact: In 2015 the LIGO detectors in the USA first caught gravitational waves, ripples in space from two black holes merging about 1.3 billion light years away.
fact: Black holes cannot be seen directly, so astronomers find them by their effects: stars orbiting nothing visible, or hot gas glowing as it falls in.
fact: Close to a black hole, time runs more slowly compared with far away, an effect predicted by Einstein.
fact: In 1974 Stephen Hawking calculated that black holes should slowly leak tiny amounts of energy and, over unimaginably long times, evaporate.
fact: Hawking's leaking, called Hawking radiation, has never been directly observed, because it is far too faint.
exercise: What is a black hole? | Mass squeezed so tight that not even light can escape | A hole in space with nothing inside it, like a tunnel to nowhere | A dead planet that has turned black | A dark cloud of dust between stars
exercise: What is the edge of a black hole called? | The event horizon | The black line | The gravity wall | The dark ring
exercise: What happens to something that crosses the event horizon? | It can never come back out | It comes out the other side in a new universe | It bounces back off | It turns into light and escapes
exercise: How do many black holes form? | Very heavy stars collapse at the end of their lives | Two planets crash together so hard that they fold into a hole | Comets fall into the Sun and pile up until it goes dark | Holes are torn in space by rockets flying much too fast
exercise: How heavy is a black hole made from a collapsing star, usually? | A few to a few dozen times the Sun's mass | Millions of times the Sun's mass, every single time | Lighter than the Moon | Exactly the mass of Earth
exercise: What sits at the centre of most large galaxies? | A supermassive black hole | A giant planet | A huge star made of ice | Nothing at all
exercise: What did the 2019 image of M87* show? | A dark centre in a glowing orange ring | A black ball with sharp spikes | A purple spiral of stars | A hole with stars falling through it into another place
exercise: Which telescope project made the first image of a black hole's shadow? | The Event Horizon Telescope | The Hubble Space Telescope | The Kepler telescope | Galileo's telescope
exercise: If the Sun became a black hole of the same mass, what would Earth do? | Keep orbiting just as before, but cold and dark | Be sucked in at once, along with all the other planets | Fly off into space, because the Sun's pull would vanish | Stop moving and just stand still where it was in its orbit
exercise: What would happen to someone falling feet-first into a small black hole? | They would be stretched out like spaghetti | They would be squashed into a ball | They would float gently inside, as if in a calm pool | They would bounce off the edge
exercise: How small would Earth have to be squeezed to become a black hole? | About 9 millimetres across, like a marble | About the size of a football | About 9 kilometres across | About the size of the Moon
exercise: What did LIGO first detect in 2015? | Gravitational waves from two black holes merging | The first ever photograph of a black hole's dark shadow | A black hole inside the solar system | Light escaping from a black hole
exercise: How do astronomers find black holes? | By their effects on stars and gas nearby | By seeing them glow black against the bright sky | By hearing them hum through big radio dishes on Earth | By sending small spacecraft right inside to take a look
exercise: What happens to time close to a black hole? | It runs more slowly than far away | It runs faster than far away | It stops for everyone everywhere | It runs backwards
exercise: What did Stephen Hawking calculate in 1974? | Black holes slowly leak energy and could evaporate | Black holes grow for ever without limit | Black holes are holes that lead into other universes | Black holes are made of dark matter
test: Leo reckons black holes go round space sucking up everything like a hoover. Is he right? | No, far away their pull is like any star of the same mass | Yes, they suck in whole galaxies from anywhere in space at once | Yes, they move towards anything near them | No, black holes have no gravity at all
test: A film shows a black hole as a bright glowing ball. What is wrong? | Light cannot escape it, so the hole itself is dark | Black holes are always bright purple, not white or yellow | Black holes are far too small to show up on a big screen | Nothing, black holes shine more brightly than any star does
test: Astronomers see a star whizzing in a tight orbit round an empty-looking spot. What might be there? | A black hole | A gap in the telescope's mirror | Nothing, stars often circle nothing | A planet bigger than the star
test: If an astronaut came back after a long stay near a black hole, how might her clock compare? | It would show less time passed than clocks on Earth | It would show more time passed, because time speeds up there | It would show exactly the same | It would show time going backwards
test: Why can't a telescope take a normal photo of the black hole itself? | No light escapes from inside it | It is always hidden behind the Sun | It is too hot to photograph | It moves too fast for a camera
test: Your little brother is scared a black hole will eat Earth tonight. What is true? | No known black hole is close enough to harm us | One is just behind the Moon | The Sun will turn into one tomorrow and pull the Earth in | Black holes eat a planet every night
test: Why could the Sun not simply turn into a black hole one day? | It is not heavy enough to collapse into one | It is too far from the galaxy's centre | It is made of the wrong kind of gas | It burns too slowly
test: Two black holes crash a billion light years away. What do we pick up on Earth? | Tiny ripples in space called gravitational waves | A loud bang heard by everyone across the whole of the Earth | A bright flash that lights up the night sky for a whole week | A strong earthquake that shakes the ground all round the Earth
test: Which is true about a supermassive black hole? | It can be billions of times the Sun's mass | It is no heavier than a planet | It only forms in empty space | It sits deep at the centre of the Earth, inside the core
test: in a game u fly into a black hole and pop out somewhere else lol. is that real | No one knows of any way out: nothing comes back across the horizon | Yes, every black hole is a tunnel that leads to a different part of space | Yes, but only small black holes | Yes, scientists have done it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the event horizon of a black hole? | It is the boundary beyond which nothing can return | It is the bright, hard surface of the black hole itself | It is the orbit of the nearest star | It is the point at which light escapes
exam: Ascertain the consequence for the Earth's orbit were the Sun replaced by a black hole of equal mass. | The Earth would continue in its orbit as before | The Earth would be drawn into the black hole at once | The Earth would be expelled from the solar system | The Earth would cease to move
exam: Identify the phenomenon first detected by the LIGO instruments in 2015. | Gravitational waves from merging black holes | Radio signals from intelligent life | The first image of a black hole's shadow | Light emitted from inside a black hole
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the means by which black holes are detected. | By their effects on nearby stars and gas | By direct observation of their black surface | By the sound they emit into space | By the light they reflect from the Sun
